What is OOPS?
Object-Oriented Programming System is a programming paradigm based on the concept of objects, 
which bundle data (variables) and behavior (methods) together.
It helps build modular, reusable, and maintainable software.

4 Main Concepts of OOPS:
1.Class: A class is a blueprint or template.
class Shoe {
    String color;
    int size;
    void wear() {}
}

2.Object: An object is an actual implementation of the class — a real entity created from the blueprint.
Shoe s1 = new Shoe();
Shoe s2 = new Shoe();

3.Encapsulation:Encapsulation means hiding internal details and exposing only what is necessary.
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;   // hidden

    public void deposit(double amount) { balance += amount; }
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
}

4.Inheritance:Inheritance means one class acquires properties of another class.
class Car {
    void start() {}
}
class ElectricCar extends Car {
    void charge() {}
}
5.Polymorphism:Polymorphism means same action, different behavior.
Compile-time polymorphism (method overloading):
void play(String song) {}
void play(String song, int volume) {}

Runtime polymorphism (method overriding):
class Animal { void sound() {} }
class Dog extends Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); } }

| OOPS Concept      | Meaning                         | Real World Analogy       |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| **Class**         | Blueprint                       | Shoe design              |
| **Object**        | Instance                        | The actual shoe you wear |
| **Encapsulation** | Hiding internal details         | ATM card + PIN system    |
| **Inheritance**   | Reuse parent features           | Car → ElectricCar        |
| **Polymorphism**  | Same action, different behavior | Smartphone power button  |

6.Abstraction:Showing only the essential features and hiding unnecessary internal details.


2.Abstraction vs Encapsulation:
Abstraction → Hiding internal implementation
Focus on: WHAT the object does
Ignore: HOW it does it

Encapsulation → Hiding data
Focus on: Protecting data by using getters/setters
Bind data + behavior together

| Feature        | Abstraction                  | Encapsulation                               |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Purpose        | Show only necessary details  | Protect data & bind together                |
| Focus          | What to do                   | How to store & secure data                  |
| Achieved using | Abstract classes, Interfaces | Classes, private variables, getters/setters |
| Visibility     | Hides implementation         | Hides data                                  |

3.Abstract Class vs Interface:
Abstract class can have : Abstract methods,Non-abstract methods,Constructors,Variables (non-final),State (fields)
When use abstract class? When subclasses share common behavior.

Interface  can have : Abstract methods,Default methods,Static methods,Constants
When use interface? When multiple classes must follow the same rule/contract.

| Feature              | Abstract Class    | Interface                                   |
| -------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Methods              | Abstract + normal | Abstract + default + static                 |
| Variables            | Can be non-final  | Always `public static final`                |
| Constructor          | Allowed           | Not allowed                                 |
| Multiple inheritance | No                | Yes (a class can implement many interfaces) |
| Use case             | Shared behavior   | Common contract                             |

4.Why Java does not allow object creation for abstract class/interface?
Abstract class → may contain unimplemented methods
Interface → contains method declarations
If object is created, calling these incomplete methods will cause runtime issues
So Java prevents it at compile time.


5.String, StringBuilder, and StringBuffer
String is immutable — like a sealed chocolate.
StringBuilder is mutable and fast — like an open jar anyone can modify.
StringBuffer is mutable and thread-safe — like a jar with a lock.

| Use Case                                                        | Use               |
| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |
| You have constant text                                          | **String**        |
| You need to build/modify text repeatedly (loops, concatenation) | **StringBuilder** |
| Same as above but in multi-threaded environment                 | **StringBuffer**  |

String Pool is a special memory area that stores string literals.
String literals with same content point to the same object.
String is immutable — this makes pooling possible.
Strings created with new are stored in the Heap, not Pool.
intern() is used to manually force a string into the pool.
== compares references; equals() compares content.

StringBuffer Methods

StringBuffer has all the same methods as StringBuilder:
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("Hello");
append() => sb.append(" World"); //Hello World
insert() => sb.insert(5, " Java");//Hello Java
replace() => sb.replace(6, 11, "Java"); // Hello World to Hello Java
delete() => sb.delete(5, 9); //Hellova
deleteCharAt() => sb.deleteCharAt(2); //abcde to abde
reverse() => sb.reverse(); //World to dlroW
capacity() => sb.capacity(); // 16 default
ensureCapacity() => sb.ensureCapacity(50); //>=50
length()=>sb.length() // returns length
charAt()=> sb.charAt(1)) // returns 1st position value

6.Exception Handling:
Exception handling is a safety mechanism that protects your program from crashing when something goes wrong.
It allows the program to continue safely instead of stopping abruptly.

try {
    // Code that may cause error
}
catch(Exception e) {
    // Handle the error
}
finally {
    // Always executed (even if error happens)
}

Types of Exceptions:
Checked Exceptions - When interacting with external resources (file, DB, network)
E.g.,
FileNotFoundException
IOException
SQLException

Unchecked Exceptions (Runtime Exceptions)-These errors occur while running the program.
When user input is unpredictable
When executing operations inside loops
To prevent crashes due to bad input
Examples:
ArithmeticException (10/0)
NullPointerException
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
NumberFormatException

Errors (Not Exceptions)
These are system-level failures.
Examples:
OutOfMemoryError
StackOverflowError
➡ You should NOT handle errors.
➡ Fix the cause instead (memory, recursion, etc.)

| Keyword / Block | Meaning                 | Analogy                     | Where Used                  |
| --------------- | ----------------------- | --------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| **final**       | Cannot change           | Sealed bottle               | Variables, Methods, Classes |
| **finally**     | Always runs             | ATM always returns card     | try-catch-finally           |
| **finalize()**  | Cleanup before deletion | Shop cleanup before closing | Garbage Collector           |

| Feature                        | `throw`                  | `throws`                     |
| ------------------------------ | ------------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| Purpose                        | Manually throw exception | Declare exception            |
| Where used?                    | Inside method            | Method signature             |
| Can throw multiple exceptions? | ❌ No (only one `throw`)  | ✔ Yes (`throws A, B, C...`)|
| When executed?                 | During runtime           | During compilation (checked) |
| Example                        | `throw new Exception();` | `void m() throws Exception`  |

